"""teach-zgn's held-out round (round 11), run after teach-zgn was closed.

teach-zgn's fix added "injective"/"image" to judson:11.1-group-homomorphisms
so it clears the decisive-margin credibility bar against
judson:16.3-ring-homomorphisms-and-ideals. It was validated only against the
fixture that found it (round10's GROUP_HOMOMORPHISMS_BLIND) and the existing
suite. This round supplies the fresh, blind check this lineage requires: one
`Agent` call with an explicit no-tool instruction (zero tool calls made),
told only four topic names, chosen to stress the fix from both sides --
group homomorphisms (the target), ring homomorphisms (judson:11.1 must not
now steal a genuine 16.3 lesson), group isomorphisms, and functions /
equivalence relations (shares "image"/"injective" with judson:1.2).

MEASURED ONCE, with the fix and with judson:11.1 reverted in memory to its
pre-fix text -- identical outcomes both ways on all four:

  GROUP_HOMOMORPHISMS_BLIND   abstains (16.3 11 vs 16.1 10, too close).
  RING_HOMOMORPHISMS_BLIND    correctly recovers judson:16.3.
  GROUP_ISOMORPHISMS_BLIND    abstains (16.3 8 vs 1.2 7, too close).
  FUNCTIONS_EQUIVALENCE_BLIND correctly recovers judson:1.2.

So the fix is safe on fresh text, but it did not do the work here:
GROUP_HOMOMORPHISMS_BLIND abstains because two WRONG nodes tie, while the
true node judson:11.1 still ranks below judson:16.3 (5 vs 11) on raw
overlap. teach-zgn's named root cause (16.3's verbose, analogy-heavy node
text out-scoring the thin 11.1 node on group-homomorphism lessons) is
still present (teach-cia); see test_true_node_still_trails_16_3_on_raw_overlap.
"""
from teach.concept_recovery import build_vocabulary_index, recover_from_lesson_text, score_candidates
from teach.judson_algebra_graph import load_judson_full_graph

GRAPH = load_judson_full_graph()
INDEX = build_vocabulary_index(GRAPH)

GROUP_HOMOMORPHISMS_BLIND = 'tutor: Today we\'re looking at group homomorphisms. They\'re how we compare one group with another. Have you run into the word yet?\nstudent: Only in passing. I think it\'s a function between groups that "respects the operation"?\ntutor: That\'s the idea. Precisely: if (G, ·) and (H, *) are groups, a map φ: G → H is a homomorphism if φ(a · b) = φ(a) * φ(b) for all a, b in G.\nstudent: So you can multiply first and then map, or map first and then multiply, and you get the same answer either way.\ntutor: Yes. Let\'s try one. Define φ: Z → Z_5 by φ(n) = n mod 5, with addition in both groups. Is that a homomorphism?\nstudent: I need φ(m + n) = φ(m) + φ(n). The remainder of m + n is the sum of the remainders, reduced mod 5. So yes.\ntutor: Good. Now a multiplicative one. Take det: GL_2(R) → R*, where R* is the nonzero reals under multiplication.\nstudent: det(AB) = det(A)det(B). That\'s exactly the homomorphism condition.\ntutor: Right. Now some consequences of the definition. What do you think φ does to the identity e_G?\nstudent: It should go to e_H, but I don\'t see why that\'s forced.\ntutor: Start with φ(e_G) = φ(e_G e_G) = φ(e_G)φ(e_G). Then multiply both sides by φ(e_G)^{-1}.\nstudent: That leaves e_H = φ(e_G). Nice. And I\'d guess inverses go to inverses?\ntutor: Check it. What is φ(a)φ(a^{-1})?\nstudent: It\'s φ(a a^{-1}) = φ(e_G) = e_H. So φ(a^{-1}) is the inverse of φ(a).\ntutor: Exactly. Now the most important object attached to a homomorphism, its kernel: ker φ = { g in G : φ(g) = e_H }.\nstudent: For the mod 5 map, the kernel is all multiples of 5, so 5Z.\ntutor: And for the determinant?\nstudent: Matrices with determinant 1. That\'s SL_2(R).\ntutor: Both kernels are subgroups. Can you show that in general?\nstudent: e_G is in it, since φ(e_G) = e_H. If a and b are in the kernel, then φ(ab^{-1}) = φ(a)φ(b)^{-1} = e_H e_H = e_H. So it\'s closed under ab^{-1}, and that\'s the one-step subgroup test.\ntutor: Very clean. In fact it\'s more than a subgroup: it\'s normal. For g in G and k in the kernel, compute φ(gkg^{-1}).\nstudent: φ(g)φ(k)φ(g)^{-1} = φ(g) e_H φ(g)^{-1} = e_H. So gkg^{-1} is in the kernel too.\ntutor: That\'s why kernels matter so much: they\'re exactly the normal subgroups you can take quotients by. One last fact. φ is injective exactly when ker φ = {e_G}.\nstudent: If φ(a) = φ(b), then φ(ab^{-1}) = e_H, so ab^{-1} is in the kernel. A trivial kernel forces a = b.\ntutor: Right, and the converse is immediate. So the mod 5 map isn\'t injective, and neither is det.\nstudent: Makes sense. Different matrices can have the same determinant.\ntutor: Exactly. To sum up: a homomorphism preserves the operation, it sends identities and inverses to identities and inverses, and its kernel is a normal subgroup that measures how far it is from injective.'

RING_HOMOMORPHISMS_BLIND = "tutor: Let's move from groups to rings. A ring homomorphism is a map φ: R → S between rings that preserves both operations.\nstudent: So φ(a + b) = φ(a) + φ(b) and φ(ab) = φ(a)φ(b)?\ntutor: Yes. Many books also require φ(1) = 1 when the rings have identities. Say which convention you're using when it matters.\nstudent: Okay. Is reduction mod n still an example, like it was for groups?\ntutor: It is. Take φ: Z → Z_n, φ(k) = k mod n. Remainders respect sums, and they respect products too.\nstudent: Right, (ab) mod n = (a mod n)(b mod n) mod n. So it's a ring homomorphism.\ntutor: Here's a second example. Fix a real number c and define ev_c: R[x] → R by ev_c(f) = f(c).\nstudent: Evaluating at c. (f + g)(c) = f(c) + g(c) and (fg)(c) = f(c)g(c), so that works as well.\ntutor: Good. Now the kernel, defined as before: ker φ = { r in R : φ(r) = 0 }. What's the kernel of the mod n map?\nstudent: nZ.\ntutor: And the kernel of ev_c?\nstudent: Polynomials with f(c) = 0. By the factor theorem, those are the multiples of (x − c).\ntutor: Exactly. Kernels of ring homomorphisms have a special property. If k is in ker φ and r is any element of R, what is φ(rk)?\nstudent: φ(r)φ(k) = φ(r) · 0 = 0. So rk is in the kernel too, even though r itself doesn't have to be.\ntutor: That absorption property is what defines an ideal. A subset I of a commutative ring R is an ideal if it's an additive subgroup and ra is in I for every r in R and a in I.\nstudent: So it's stronger than being a subring. It swallows products with anything in the ring.\ntutor: Right. Is Z an ideal of Q?\nstudent: It's an additive subgroup, but (1/2) · 1 = 1/2 isn't in Z. So no.\ntutor: Correct. Now check that nZ is an ideal of Z directly.\nstudent: nZ is a subgroup under addition. If r is in Z and nk is in nZ, then r(nk) = n(rk), which is in nZ.\ntutor: Good. So every kernel is an ideal. It also goes the other way: every ideal I is the kernel of the quotient map R → R/I, r ↦ r + I.\nstudent: So ideals play the same role for rings that normal subgroups play for groups.\ntutor: Exactly. That leads to the First Isomorphism Theorem for rings: R / ker φ ≅ φ(R).\nstudent: For ev_c, it's surjective, since constant polynomials hit every real number. So R[x]/(x − c) ≅ R.\ntutor: Right. Last question: is ev_c injective?\nstudent: No. The kernel (x − c) isn't just {0}, and x − c is a nonzero polynomial that maps to 0.\ntutor: Well done. So a ring homomorphism preserves addition and multiplication, and its kernel is an ideal, which is what lets us form quotient rings."

GROUP_ISOMORPHISMS_BLIND = 'tutor: Today\'s topic is group isomorphisms, which make precise the idea of two groups being "the same."\nstudent: Is an isomorphism just a homomorphism that\'s also a bijection?\ntutor: That\'s the definition. φ: G → H is an isomorphism if it\'s a bijective homomorphism, and then we write G ≅ H.\nstudent: So the elements get renamed, but the multiplication table stays the same.\ntutor: That\'s a good way to think about it. Here\'s an example: (R, +) and (R_{>0}, ·). Try φ(x) = e^x.\nstudent: e^{x+y} = e^x e^y, so it\'s a homomorphism. It\'s a bijection onto the positive reals because ln is its inverse.\ntutor: So addition of reals and multiplication of positive reals are the same group in disguise. Here\'s a finite one: is Z_6 ≅ Z_2 × Z_3?\nstudent: Both have order 6. Does that settle it?\ntutor: No. Having the same order is necessary but not sufficient. Look for a generator of Z_2 × Z_3 instead.\nstudent: Try (1, 1). Its multiples are (1,1), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,2), (0,0). That\'s six distinct elements, so it\'s cyclic.\ntutor: Right. So define φ: Z_6 → Z_2 × Z_3 by φ(k) = k · (1, 1) = (k mod 2, k mod 3).\nstudent: That respects addition, and it\'s a bijection because (1, 1) generates. So they\'re isomorphic.\ntutor: Now the opposite case: is Z_4 ≅ Z_2 × Z_2?\nstudent: Also order 4 on both sides. I can\'t find an isomorphism, but I\'m not sure how to prove that none exists.\ntutor: Use invariants. An isomorphism preserves element orders: if φ is an isomorphism, then |φ(g)| = |g|.\nstudent: Why is that?\ntutor: φ(g)^n = φ(g^n), and φ(g^n) = e exactly when g^n = e, because φ is injective. So g and φ(g) are killed by exactly the same n.\nstudent: Then Z_4 has an element of order 4, namely 1. In Z_2 × Z_2, every nonidentity element has order 2.\ntutor: So there\'s no isomorphism. What other properties must two isomorphic groups share?\nstudent: Probably being abelian, and being cyclic.\ntutor: Yes. Here\'s a quick check: are Z_6 and S_3 isomorphic?\nstudent: S_3 isn\'t abelian. (1 2)(1 3) and (1 3)(1 2) are different permutations. Z_6 is abelian, so they\'re not isomorphic.\ntutor: Right. One more property. If φ is an isomorphism, then its inverse function is also a homomorphism.\nstudent: Let a = φ(x) and b = φ(y). Then φ^{-1}(ab) = φ^{-1}(φ(xy)) = xy = φ^{-1}(a)φ^{-1}(b).\ntutor: That\'s why ≅ behaves like an equivalence relation on groups. To sum up: to prove two groups are isomorphic, build a bijective homomorphism. To prove they aren\'t, find a property one has that the other doesn\'t.'

FUNCTIONS_EQUIVALENCE_BLIND = "tutor: Let's go back to foundations: functions and equivalence relations. They'll come up in every chapter after this one.\nstudent: I know a function takes an input and gives an output. What's the formal definition?\ntutor: A function f: A → B is a subset of A × B in which every a in A appears in exactly one pair (a, b). We write f(a) = b.\nstudent: So every input has an output, and no input has two outputs.\ntutor: Right. Two key properties. f is injective if f(a_1) = f(a_2) implies a_1 = a_2. It's surjective if every b in B equals f(a) for some a.\nstudent: And bijective means both.\ntutor: Yes. Let's test f: Z → Z, f(n) = 2n + 1.\nstudent: If 2m + 1 = 2n + 1, then m = n, so it's injective. It's not surjective, though, since 2 is never hit because the outputs are all odd.\ntutor: Good. What about g: R → R, g(x) = 2x + 1?\nstudent: Now it is surjective: for any y, take x = (y − 1)/2. So g is a bijection.\ntutor: So the same formula can behave differently depending on the domain. Now for relations. A relation on A is any subset of A × A. It's an equivalence relation if it's reflexive, symmetric and transitive.\nstudent: Reflexive means a ~ a. Symmetric means a ~ b implies b ~ a. Transitive means a ~ b and b ~ c implies a ~ c.\ntutor: Exactly. The standard example: on Z, a ~ b if n divides a − b. That's congruence mod n.\nstudent: Reflexive, because n divides 0. Symmetric, because if n divides a − b, it divides b − a too.\ntutor: Now transitivity.\nstudent: If a − b = nk and b − c = nm, then a − c = n(k + m). So it holds.\ntutor: Good. A non-example: is ≤ on R an equivalence relation?\nstudent: It's reflexive and transitive, but 1 ≤ 2 and 2 ≤ 1 is false. It's not symmetric.\ntutor: Right. Now the equivalence class of a: [a] = { x in A : x ~ a }. What are the classes for congruence mod 3?\nstudent: [0] is the multiples of 3, [1] is the numbers of the form 3k + 1, and [2] is the numbers of the form 3k + 2. Everything is in exactly one of them.\ntutor: That's the main theorem here: the equivalence classes of any equivalence relation partition A. They're nonempty, they cover A, and any two of them are either equal or disjoint.\nstudent: Why can't two classes overlap without being equal?\ntutor: Suppose c is in both [a] and [b]. Then c ~ a and c ~ b. What does that give you?\nstudent: By symmetry a ~ c, and then transitivity gives a ~ b. So anything related to a is related to b, which makes [a] = [b].\ntutor: Exactly. One more example: on R^2, let (x_1, y_1) ~ (x_2, y_2) if x_1^2 + y_1^2 = x_2^2 + y_2^2.\nstudent: That's an equivalence relation because it's defined by equality of a quantity. The classes are circles centered at the origin, plus the origin itself.\ntutor: Good. In general, if f: A → B is any function, then a ~ a' when f(a) = f(a') is an equivalence relation, and its classes are the fibers of f.\nstudent: So functions and equivalence relations are linked. Here f is the squared distance from the origin.\ntutor: That's the takeaway: every function induces an equivalence relation on its domain, and every equivalence relation splits its set into disjoint classes."

CASES = [
    (GROUP_HOMOMORPHISMS_BLIND, "judson:11.1-group-homomorphisms"),
    (RING_HOMOMORPHISMS_BLIND, "judson:16.3-ring-homomorphisms-and-ideals"),
    (GROUP_ISOMORPHISMS_BLIND, "judson:9.1-definition-and-examples"),
    (FUNCTIONS_EQUIVALENCE_BLIND, "judson:1.2-sets-and-equivalence-relations"),
]


def test_no_case_is_a_confident_wrong_answer():
    for text, true_id in CASES:
        got = recover_from_lesson_text(text, GRAPH).taught_node_id
        assert got in (true_id, None), f"{true_id}: confident wrong answer {got}"


def test_the_fix_does_not_steal_a_genuine_ring_homomorphisms_lesson():
    got = recover_from_lesson_text(RING_HOMOMORPHISMS_BLIND, GRAPH).taught_node_id
    assert got == "judson:16.3-ring-homomorphisms-and-ideals"


def test_true_node_still_trails_16_3_on_raw_overlap():
    """Pins teach-zgn's residual root cause. If this starts failing because
    judson:11.1 outscores judson:16.3, the root cause was addressed -- flip
    it and say so."""
    scores = {c.node_id: c.score for c in score_candidates(GROUP_HOMOMORPHISMS_BLIND, INDEX)}
    assert scores["judson:11.1-group-homomorphisms"] < scores["judson:16.3-ring-homomorphisms-and-ideals"]
